// motes — bioluminescent particles that entrain. funideas §6's `synchronise`,
// and the thing it promises: "thousands of bioluminescent particles whose
// emission phases gradually synchronise locally. You'd get travelling waves of
// light emerging almost accidentally."
//
// ⚠ NOT the same effect as matryoshka's `kernels/motes.rill`, which is dust in
// a sun shaft. That repo's entrainment kernel is called `fireflies.rill`. Same
// filename, different effects — check which repo you opened.
//
// A cloud of blinking specks whose blink rate rises across x, so one end runs
// faster than the other, each with a random offset on top. Each pulls gently
// on whoever is in reach, so local patches fall into step and bands of
// synchronised blinking drift across the cloud.
//
// Nothing here makes a wave. Every row is a lone oscillator with its own
// natural frequency and a whisper of coupling to whatever is within reach.
// The wave is what a FIELD of them does.
//
// ⚠ What a phase LOOKS like is not this file's business: `row.u0` is a number
// in [0, 1) and the spray's appearance decides whether that reads as a hard
// blink, a ramp or a glow.
//
//   row.u0  phase    [0, 1), what the renderer lights
//   row.u1  drift    this row's own frequency, cycles a second. (Nothing to do
//                    with `plane.drift`, which is the path head every kernel
//                    reads its knobs from, or with drift-run.)
//   row.u3  crowd    how many rows are in reach — written for a reader,
//                    nothing here consumes it
//
// `near` leaves the list `sync` reads, so the ORDER of the last two lines
// matters: move the `near` and the coupling stops.
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/motes.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.slope   =<Hz per cell of x>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.base    =<Hz at x = 0>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.jitter  =<Hz, per-row spread>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.reach   =<radius, cells>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.couple  =<how hard neighbours pull>
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).
describe motes
    "Blinking specks that fall into step with their neighbours. Each blinks at its own rate, quicker toward one end of the cloud than the other, and a gentle pull between rows in reach gathers them into bands of synchronised blinking."

spawn
perish

// The frequency has a gradient along x — the far end of the cloud runs
// faster than the near end — plus a per-row jitter, because `row.seed` is
// already a uniform in [0, 1) and disorder is what the coupling has to
// beat. A gradient alone would be a wave nobody had to work for.
row.pos.x | mul plane.drift.@self.k.slope | add plane.drift.@self.k.base | write row.u1
row.seed | mul plane.drift.@self.k.jitter | write row.u1 add

// Who is close, and lean toward them.
near plane.drift.@self.k.reach | write row.u3
sync row.u0 row.u1 plane.drift.@self.k.couple
